1
00:00:52,526 --> 00:00:56,081
<v Helen>So, Brad, what did you think of the article on group work?


2
00:00:56,081 --> 00:01:04,286
<v Brad>Oh hi, Helen ... yeah it was pretty good ... with helpful pieces of advice on how to make group work effective.


3
00:01:04,286 --> 00:01:10,844
<v Helen>I think we were lucky to be given such a straightforward text to present at the management skills seminar.


4
00:01:10,844 --> 00:01:15,459
<v Brad>Yeah ... actually shall we discuss it now ... have you got time?


5
00:01:15,449 --> 00:01:24,477
<v Helen>Sure ... it’s only a ten-minute presentation, so we just need to explain and then give our views on the main points raised in the article.


6
00:01:24,467 --> 00:01:27,067
<v Brad>I’ll jot down some notes ... Right.


7
00:01:27,067 --> 00:01:32,875
<v Helen>So, there are three main sections. I suggest we start with listening.


8
00:01:32,875 --> 00:01:41,059
<v Brad>Yeah ... effective listening in groups ... because it’s not something that’s
frequently covered on courses in our field …


9
00:01:41,059 --> 00:01:44,566
<v Helen>No ... and we should say that in the presentation.


10
00:01:44,576 --> 00:01:50,164
<v Brad>Yeah ... And also effective listening’s pretty simple, you know, I don’t think it’s hard to learn. 


11
00:01:50,164 --> 00:01:57,447
<v Helen> Well ... people think it’s easy, but in my experience most of us tend to be very lazy listeners.

12
00:01:57,437 --> 00:01:59,787
<v Brad> OK - I wouldn’t argue with that.


13
00:01:59,787 --> 00:02:09,472
<v Helen>Something I do think we should emphasise is the power of the listener’s
posture, gestures, etc. in making speakers feel respected.


14
00:02:09,472 --> 00:02:09,572
<v Brad>... not that you’re just waiting for them to finish.before jumping in with your own
ideas …


15
00:02:14,622 --> 00:02:15,310
<v Helen>Ah ha.


16
00:02:15,310 --> 00:02:21,797
<v Brad>Right ... the next section is on goal setting - let’s make sure we’re clear what the
article says on this.


17
00:02:21,797 --> 00:02:28,755
<v Helen>Yeah - well, firstly it says that all group members must be given time to
explain their own goals.


18
00:02:28,755 --> 00:02:29,780
<v Brad>... that’s it, yeah.


19
00:02:29,780 --> 00:02:34,177
<v Helen>... and then did it say that the whole group should agree on common goals?


20
00:02:34,177 --> 00:02:47,049
<v Helen>That’s a bit too strong. It’s more that everyone’s agendas should be equally acceptable ... but it does say that goals have to be realistic, you know ..... achievable within a particular time?


21
00:02:47,039 --> 00:02:58,255
<v Brad>You’ve got it. That’s really what the article’s saying. There isn’t really any point in having ‘ideals’ if group members know they won’t come to anything within a reasonable period …


22
00:02:58,255 --> 00:03:04,295
<v Brad>So ... I think a summary covering those points will be enough for that part of the presentation, don’t you?


23
00:03:04,285 --> 00:03:08,739
<v Helen>Yep ... Now the last section is about conflict resolution.


24
00:03:08,739 --> 00:03:11,495
<v Brad>Actually, I thought it was the worst part of the article.


25
00:03:11,495 --> 00:03:12,130
<v Helen>Me too .


26
00:03:12,130 --> 00:03:15,587
<v Brad>I don’t think it went into sufficient detail on the issue. 

27
00:03:15,587 --> 00:03:21,717
<v Helen>Actually I thought it devoted too much space to it but that it was all rather boring, you know.

28
00:03:21,707 --> 00:03:24,610
<v Brad>It didn’t mention some of the more radical theories ... 

29
00:03:24,610 --> 00:03:27,611
<v Helen>... absolutely .I found that really irritating.


30
00:03:27,601 --> 00:03:33,419
<v Brad>Right ... and also I think it could have said more about conflict sometimes being healthy in groups …


31
00:03:33,419 --> 00:03:43,541
<v Brad>Absolutely ... it just mentioned rather glibly about how we should avoid thinking of winners and losers and that quick resolution of conflict is always desirable.


32
00:03:43,541 --> 00:03:45,797
<v Brad> Without explaining what these terms mean ... ?


33
00:03:45,797 --> 00:03:52,318
<v Helen>Well, it gives quite detailed definitions but doesn’t develop a proper argument.


34
00:03:52,318 --> 00:03:57,521
<v Brad>Right ... So for the presentation, I think we just give some definitions and ...

35
00:03:57,521 --> 00:04:04,346
<v Helen>... and then explain what we felt were the weaknesses in the article’s treatment of conflict resolution.


36
00:04:04,346 --> 00:04:05,277
<v Brad> Yeah ... good.


37
00:04:35,767 --> 00:04:39,765
<v Brad> So, let’s think about what we have to prepare for the actual presentation.


38
00:04:39,765 --> 00:04:52,918
<v Helen> Well, I suppose we’ll use PowerPoint ... but I’m hopeless at using it, especially if it has any visuals. I really have to look into doing a course on it because I know I’ll need it in the future.


39
00:04:52,918 --> 00:04:57,713
<v Brad> Don’t worry, I’m quite happy using PowerPoint and I’ll put it together when everything else is ready.


40
00:04:57,713 --> 00:05:00,998
<v Helen> That’s a relief ... but, yes, do that later.


41
00:05:00,998 --> 00:05:06,872
<v Brad> OK. Now, I heard the tutor saying we have to include some well chosen quotations from the article?


42
00:05:06,872 --> 00:05:10,587
<v Helen>I’m not sure if we do ... I’ll email him to find out.


43
00:05:10,587 --> 00:05:14,694
<v Brad> No need, I can just have a look at the specs he gave us when he set the task ...


44
00:05:14,694 --> 00:05:15,715
<v Helen> That’ll be quicker.


45
00:05:15,715 --> 00:05:22,965
<v Brad> But the tutor definitely said we have to prepare a handout to go with the talk ... I’m not really sure how we do that.


46
00:05:22,955 --> 00:05:23,055
<v Brad> But the tutor definitely said we have to prepare a handout to go with the talk ... I’m not really sure how we do that.


47
